# Corner Bagel — the environment.
#
# Copy to .env.local for a local run; set the same names in Render for the
# deployment. Nothing here has a default, and nothing here is optional if you
# want the feature next to it to work.
#
# One rule, and it is the only one that matters: no name in this file starts
# with NEXT_PUBLIC_. That prefix means "compile this value into the JavaScript
# every visitor downloads", and every value here is a secret that stays on the
# server. The one key a browser does see, Google's, is served at request time
# by /api/maps-config instead, so setting it is a restart and not a rebuild.


# ——— Auth0 · sign in and sign up ———
#
# Passwordless email codes. There is no password anywhere in this app, so
# there is none to leak and none to reset.
#
# In the Auth0 dashboard the application needs:
#   · the Passwordless OTP grant enabled (Application → Advanced → Grant Types)
#   · an Email passwordless connection turned on and attached to it
#   · Attack Protection on, which is the real rate limiter — ours is in-memory
#
# AUTH0_ISSUER_BASE_URL has no trailing slash: https://your-tenant.us.auth0.com
AUTH0_ISSUER_BASE_URL=
AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=
AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Signs our own session cookie — ours, not Auth0's. 32+ random bytes:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Changing it signs everybody out, which is also how you sign everybody out.
AUTH0_SESSION_SECRET=


# ——— Toast · the menu, the till, and card payment ———
#
# Orders go onto the POS through this. Without it an order is logged on the
# server and nothing reaches the kitchen.
#
# TOAST_API_HOST is the environment's base URL — the sandbox and production
# hosts differ, and this is the one switch between them.
TOAST_API_HOST=
TOAST_CLIENT_ID=
TOAST_CLIENT_SECRET=
TOAST_RESTAURANT_GUID=


# ——— Google Maps Platform · the map, address search, geocoding, routing ———
#
# One key does all four, which is why this replaced Radar. Enable these four
# APIs on the project in the Cloud console, and enable billing, or every one of
# them answers REQUEST_DENIED:
#
#   Maps JavaScript API   draws the map on /locations
#   Places API (New)      address autocomplete as somebody types
#   Geocoding API         address text to coordinates, server side
#   Routes API            driving distance and time from the shop, server side
#
# Set GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY and you are done. It is used for the server calls and
# also served to the browser by /api/maps-config, which is how the Maps library
# authenticates. That works, and it is what a first deployment looks like.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
#
# Optional, and the safer setup. A key that is served to browsers should be
# restricted by HTTP referrer to your domains, but a referrer restriction would
# reject the server's own calls, which carry no referrer. So: make a second key,
# restrict it by referrer, limit it to Maps JavaScript and Places, and put it
# here. The browser gets this one; GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY then never leaves the
# server and can be locked down to Geocoding and Routes.
GOOGLE_MAPS_BROWSER_KEY=
#
# The diagnostics are behind DIAGNOSTIC_TOKEN, further down. /api/maps-check is
# the one that answers for these keys specifically — it calls Geocoding, Routes
# and Places for real and reports what Google said.


# ——— Which map draws the basemap ———
#
# Two options, and only the basemap changes. Address search, geocoding and
# routing are Google either way, and their key stays where it is.
#
#   google      The default. Nothing to host. Draws its own logo and
#               "Map data ©" and Terms along the base of the canvas, and the
#               Maps Platform terms forbid modifying or obscuring any of it.
#               Billed per map load.
#
#   protomaps   OpenStreetMap data as one .pmtiles file on your own storage,
#               drawn by MapLibre. No key, no per-load bill, and the whole
#               attribution requirement is a small pill reading
#               "Protomaps © OpenStreetMap".
#
# Whatever this says, ?map=google and ?map=protomaps on /locations override it
# for that visit, so the two can be compared on one deployment.
MAP_PROVIDER=google
#
# Only read when the provider is protomaps. Build an extract for the area you
# serve at build.protomaps.com, then put the file somewhere that answers HTTP
# range requests with CORS open: R2, S3 and B2 all do, and plenty of static
# hosts do not. That is the whole trick, MapLibre reads the few kilobytes it
# needs rather than downloading a multi-gigabyte file.
PMTILES_URL=
#
# Fonts and icons for the map's labels, which vector tiles don't carry. Both
# default to Protomaps' public asset host, which is static files rather than an
# API: no key, no account, no per-request bill. Set them to copies you serve
# yourself if you'd rather owe nobody anything.
MAP_GLYPHS_URL=
MAP_SPRITE_URL=


# ——— Uber Direct · the courier ———
#
# Quotes what a delivery costs for a given address, and books the driver.
# The customer ID is the UUID from the Uber Direct dashboard, not your Uber
# account. The application needs the eats.deliveries scope.
UBER_DIRECT_CUSTOMER_ID=
UBER_DIRECT_CLIENT_ID=
UBER_DIRECT_CLIENT_SECRET=


# ——— Anthropic · Riley, the chat ———
#
# Without it the chat window says it can't answer and points at the shop's
# email, rather than sitting there silently.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=


# ——— Loops · the drop list ———
#
# The email signup on the marketing pages. Adding the contact is also what
# fires the welcome email, which is a Loops Workflow configured in their
# dashboard rather than anything this app sends — so the workflow has to be
# switched on there too, or people join a list and hear nothing.
#
# Without the key the signup still accepts the address and logs it; nobody is
# actually subscribed.
LOOPS_API_KEY=


# ——— Loops · job applications ———
#
# The ID of a Loops transactional template. /api/apply renders the application
# as a PDF and sends it as an attachment on this template, so the template
# itself only needs to be the covering note. It receives these data variables:
#
#   applicantName, applicantEmail, applicantPhone, positions
#   summary        every answer as plain text, newline separated. Put it in a
#                  monospaced or pre-wrap block. When the application was
#                  translated this holds the English first and the applicant's
#                  own words underneath, so it is where you check what they
#                  actually wrote.
#   translatedFrom the language it came from, or empty when it was English.
#   pdfIncomplete  "yes" when the PDF's font could not draw something, "no"
#                  otherwise. Rare now that the document is English, but a
#                  translation outage can still leave the original in it.
#
# ⚠️ Attachments have to be enabled on the Loops account by their support. The
# API accepts the call without it and silently drops the file, which looks
# exactly like success — so send one to yourself before trusting it.
#
# With no key or no template ID the endpoint still validates and renders, logs
# a warning saying the application was NOT mailed, and answers ok. That is for
# local development; in production an unset value means applications are lost.
LOOPS_APPLICATION_TRANSACTIONAL_ID=

# Where applications are delivered. Defaults to abu@thecornerbagel.com.
CAREERS_INBOX=

# ——— Job applications in English ———
#
# The form is offered in ten languages; the document that reaches CAREERS_INBOX
# is always in English. Any application not written in English is put through
# one translation pass before the PDF is drawn, using ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (the
# same key Riley uses — there is no second key to set).
#
# The applicant's own words are never discarded: the covering email carries the
# English first and the original underneath, and the PDF says on its first line
# that it is a translation and what from.
#
# If the key is missing or the call fails, the application still goes out — in
# the language it was written in, with the PDF saying so. Nothing is ever lost
# to a translation outage.
#
# Optional override, mainly for trading accuracy against latency. The endpoint
# waits for this before it answers, so a faster model is a faster form.
APPLY_TRANSLATE_MODEL=


# ——— The shop's own phone ———
#
# E.164, e.g. +12135551234. This is the number Uber's courier calls when
# they're at the counter or at the door, so a wrong one is a delivery stalled
# on the pavement. There is a placeholder in shopFacts.ts — set this.
SHOP_PHONE=


# ——— Postgres ———
#
# Two tables: push subscriptions, and the kitchen queue that feeds the "4
# orders ahead" line under the Order button. Everything else in this app gets
# by without a database — orders live in Toast, the cart and the order history
# live in the browser — so this is optional, and both features simply say they
# do not know when it is absent.
#
# ——— Sharing one with another project is fine ———
#
# Every table this app creates lives in a schema of its own, named and written
# out in full on every statement:
#
#   corner_bagel.push_subscriptions
#   corner_bagel.kitchen_queue
#
# So a database that already has a table called push_subscriptions is not a
# problem. It is worth saying why, because in `public` it would be a quiet one:
# CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS against an existing name does nothing, reports
# success, and leaves every query afterwards running against somebody else's
# columns — an INSERT into the wrong table rather than a crash.
#
# Taking this app back off a shared database is one statement:
#
#   DROP SCHEMA corner_bagel CASCADE;
#
# Two things to check before sharing one:
#
#   connections   the pool here opens up to 4 per web instance. Postgres
#                 refuses new connections rather than slowing down when a plan
#                 runs out, which takes both projects down rather than one.
#   lifecycle     deleting or migrating the other project takes this with it,
#                 and backups are shared.
#
# ——— Which variable ———
#
# CORNER_DATABASE_URL if it is set, DATABASE_URL otherwise.
#
# Use CORNER_DATABASE_URL when DATABASE_URL is already taken. On a Render
# account running more than one thing it usually is, and the value in it is
# usually right for whatever put it there — overwriting that to suit this app
# trades one broken feature for another somewhere nobody is looking.
CORNER_DATABASE_URL=
#
# The default, for a deployment where nothing else has claimed the name.
DATABASE_URL=
#
# ⚠️ Render gives a managed Postgres two connection strings, and only one of
# them works from a service that is not sitting next to it:
#
#   internal   dpg-xxxx-a                              no domain. Resolves only
#                                                      from the same region and
#                                                      account. Anywhere else:
#                                                      getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND.
#   external   dpg-xxxx-a.oregon-postgres.render.com   works from anywhere.
#
# ENOTFOUND on a bare dpg- host means the internal one is in use somewhere it
# cannot reach. TLS is handled either way — see the note in app/db.ts about
# Render's certificate authority.


# ——— Diagnostics ———
#
# Set this to any random string and two endpoints answer; leave it unset and
# both 404, which is where they should spend their lives.
#
#   /api/status?token=<it>       every integration: on or off, and what is
#                                lost while it is off. Postgres and Toast are
#                                asked for real; the rest report whether the
#                                variables are set.
#   /api/maps-check?token=<it>   calls Geocoding, Routes and Places, and for
#                                any that refuse, prints what Google said.
#
# They exist because every integration in this app fails softly on purpose. No
# database and the queue says it does not know; no Toast and orders are logged;
# no Resend and an application is written to a log. All correct, and all
# invisible from outside — broken looks exactly like working, and the Google
# keys stayed broken for weeks on exactly that.
#
# Neither ever reports a key, a host, a connection string, or a length.
#
# MAPS_DIAGNOSTIC_TOKEN is still read as an older name for the same thing.
DIAGNOSTIC_TOKEN=


# ——— ⚠️ Testing outside opening hours ———
#
# The counter runs 7am–4pm Pacific, which is a narrow window to do integration
# work in: the delivery quote, the courier booking, the Toast ticket, the queue
# count and the tracker can only be exercised end to end while the shop is
# open. This moves closing time so that can happen in an evening.
#
#   SHOP_OPEN_PREVIEW=21   the counter closes at 9pm today instead of 4pm
#   SHOP_OPEN_PREVIEW=1    open at every hour of every day
#
# ⚠️ Either way the app will take an order the kitchen is not there to make.
# The customer is told their breakfast is being prepared, a courier is booked
# and arrives at a dark shop, and the first anybody knows is a complaint.
#
# Prefer the hour. It bounds the damage — the shop still opens at 7am, so
# nothing is orderable at 3am — and it expires on its own. A `1` sits in an
# environment until somebody remembers it is there, which is how a test
# setting becomes a production one; `21` stops mattering at 9pm whether or not
# anybody comes back to remove it.
#
# Anything that is not an hour between 8 and 24 falls through to the real
# hours, so a typo closes the shop rather than opening it.
SHOP_OPEN_PREVIEW=
